  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why is the overhead wire such a high voltage?",
      "a": "A high voltage lets the line carry lots of power with a smaller current, so less energy is lost as heat and the wire can be thinner. Most modern main lines use 25,000 volts AC.",
      "asks": [
        "How many volts is the overhead line?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a pantograph?",
      "a": "A pantograph is the folding frame on the roof of an electric train. Its top carries a carbon strip that slides along the overhead wire and collects the current.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the thing on top of an electric train?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What keeps the pantograph touching the wire?",
      "a": "Springs or air pressure push the pantograph up with a steady, gentle force. Too little and it would bounce and spark; too much and it would wear the wire.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a pantograph spark?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does an electric train need a transformer?",
      "a": "The motors cannot use 25,000 volts directly. A transformer on board steps the voltage down, and power electronics then control the current to the motors.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the transformer on a train do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the electricity get back?",
      "a": "Current flows from the wire through the train's equipment and motors, then out through the wheels into the steel rails, which carry it back to the substation.",
      "asks": [
        "Do the rails carry electricity?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Most modern high-speed and main lines use 25,000 volts AC overhead power.",
    "The overhead wire is staggered from side to side so it wears the pantograph strip evenly.",
    "Overhead wires are kept tight by heavy weights or springs so they do not sag in hot weather."
  ],
